<node id="Sepsis and Systemic Inflammatory Response">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Sepsis triggers widespread inflammation, capillary leak, coagulation disturbances, and organ injury. Surviving sepsis is a major determinant of ICU outcomes. Markers such as CRP, WBC, lactic acid, and the 'Sepsis3' variable indicate systemic inflammation and bacterial burden.</data>
  <data key="d2">36</data>
</node>
<node id="NBPsmmHg">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="NBPdmmHg">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="CVPmmHg">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="HCO3serum">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="FiO2">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Aniongap">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Hemoglobingdl">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Acute Respiratory Distress and Hypoxemia">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Dysfunction in gas exchange (e.g., ARDS, severe pneumonia) causes hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and impaired ventilatory mechanics. Variables like PaO2, FiO2, PaO2/FiO2, SpO2, respiratory rate, PEEP, and compliance represent the severity and effectiveness of respiratory support.</data>
  <data key="d2">29</data>
</node>
<node id="Hematocritserum">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Cardiovascular Instability and Shock">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Shock—whether septic, cardiogenic, or hypovolemic—results in tissue hypoperfusion and is reflected by features such as hypotension (ABP, NBPs, NBPd), central venous pressure, heart rate, requirement for vasoactive medications (epinephrine, norepinephrine, dobutamine), and blood lactate. These illustrate attempts to maintain adequate organ perfusion in the face of circulatory collapse.</data>
  <data key="d2">32</data>
</node>
<node id="Epinephrinemg_avg">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Dobutaminemg_avg">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Norepinephrinemg_avg">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Neurological Impairment and Coma">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Altered consciousness due to brain injury, metabolic derangement, or excessive sedation is a predictor of adverse outcomes. The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS), including its eye, verbal, and motor components, captures the extent of neurological impairment.</data>
  <data key="d2">30</data>
</node>
<node id="Calciumnon-ionized">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="IonizedCalcium">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Metabolic Dysregulation and Electrolyte Imbalances">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Critical illness is often accompanied by disturbances in glucose handling, acid-base status, and electrolytes. Features such as glucose, sodium, potassium, calcium, bicarbonate (HCO3), anion gap, pH, and base excess are essential for detecting life-threatening derangements.</data>
  <data key="d2">32</data>
</node>
<node id="PCO2VenousmmHg">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Comorbidities and Patient Characteristics">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Baseline vulnerabilities such as advanced age, gender, pre-existing diabetes, hypertension, heart failure, and obesity markedly heighten the risk of ICU mortality by reducing physiological reserves and increasing susceptibility to complications.</data>
  <data key="d2">30</data>
</node>
<node id="gender">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="NSURG">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="NMED">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="Coagulopathy and Hematologic Abnormalities">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Severe illness may induce coagulopathy (impaired clotting or excessive bleeding), anemia, or thrombocytopenia, increasing the risk of poor tissue oxygenation, bleeding, or thrombotic complications. These are reflected in values such as platelet count, hemoglobin, hematocrit, PTT, and INR.</data>
  <data key="d2">29</data>
</node>
<node id="Service Type and Clinical Pathways">
  <data key="d0">mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">Different ICU service types (medical, surgical, trauma, etc.) reflect both the underlying illness burden and the nature of interventions. These categories may proxy for unmeasured severity or risks intrinsic to the admitting diagnosis and relate to different mortality risks.</data>
  <data key="d2">30</data>
</node>
<node id="TSURG">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="CMED">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="VSURG">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
<node id="TRAUM">
  <data key="d0">feature</data>
</node>
